CNS — LOCAL LLM PATH ARCHITECTURE

Final Architecture Log

Status: LOCKED — Architecture Design Phase


---

1. Core Decision

CNS will not be architecturally tied to any particular LLM family.

Qwen, DeepSeek, GLM, Kimi, Mistral, Gemma, or future open models are all interchangeable model candidates.

CNS must be designed around a model-agnostic Local Model Bus.

> Build the road, not the vehicle.



The model can change without redesigning CNS.


---

2. CNS Local Model Highway

The fundamental path is:

CNS
 │
 ▼
Local Model Bus
 │
 ▼
Model Registry
 │
 ▼
Model / Capability Router
 │
 ▼
Model Manager
 │
 ▼
Runtime Adapter
 │
 ▼
CPU / RAM / GPU
 │
 ▼
Selected Local Model

Every compatible local model must enter CNS through this path.


---

3. Model Registry

CNS maintains a registry describing every local model available to it.

Each model entry should contain information such as:

Model Family
Model Name
Version
Quantization
Model Size
Runtime Compatibility
RAM Requirement
VRAM Requirement
CPU Requirement
Context Capability
Reasoning Capability
Tool Calling
Structured Output
Vision / Multimodal Capability
Current Health
Preferred Tasks

The registry is metadata, not the model itself.

This allows CNS to decide:

> "Which model can perform this task on the hardware currently available?"



instead of hard-coding a model name.


---

4. Local Model Warehouse

The CNS server will contain a local model library.

The model files live on the server's NVMe/SSD storage.

Example:

/models/

    qwen/
        model-A-Q4
        model-B-Q5

    deepseek/
        model-A-Q4
        model-B-Q8

    glm/
        model-A-Q4

    future-model/
        ...

Multiple quantized models can therefore be installed on the same server.

Important distinction

SSD / NVMe
    =
Model Warehouse

RAM
    =
Active Workspace

We can have many models stored on disk while only loading the model(s) needed at that moment into RAM.


---

5. Model Manager

The Model Manager controls which local model is actually loaded.

Example:

User:
"Use local DeepSeek."
        │
        ▼
Model Manager
        │
        ├── Is DeepSeek installed?
        │
        ├── Does it fit available resources?
        │
        ├── Which quantization?
        │
        └── Which runtime?
        │
        ▼
Load model
        │
        ▼
Run task

If another model is already occupying RAM, the manager can unload it when appropriate.

Therefore:

> Installed does not mean permanently loaded.




---

6. Three Universal Model-Switching Paths

This is one of the most important CNS design decisions.

PATH A — Same model, different compute home

Model X
   │
   ├── Home A
   ├── Home B
   ├── Home C
   └── Local Home

Example:

Qwen
 │
 ├── Provider A ❌ quota
 ├── Provider B ✅
 └── Local fallback

The model remains Qwen.

Only the compute location changes.


---

PATH B — Same family, different version / size / quantization

Model Family X
      │
      ├── Version A
      ├── Version B
      ├── Larger model
      ├── Smaller model
      └── Different quantization

Example:

Qwen large model
      ↓ unavailable
Qwen smaller model
      ↓
Qwen local quantized model

The model family remains the same.


---

PATH C — Different model family

Model Family A
      ↓ unavailable / unsuitable
Model Family B
      ↓
Model Family C

Example:

Qwen
 ↓
DeepSeek
 ↓
GLM

This happens according to CNS's capability and policy rules.


---

7. Chat-Controlled Model Selection

The model path must be controllable through normal CNS chat.

Examples:

"Use local Qwen."

"Use local DeepSeek."

"Use the best available local model."

"Use only local models."

"Don't use paid models."

"Switch the local brain to DeepSeek."

"Use whatever local model is best for this task."

CNS should translate these instructions into Model Manager / Router operations.

The user should not need to manually edit configuration files every time they want to change models.


---

8. Automatic Model Selection

If the user doesn't specify a model, CNS should select one automatically.

Selection should consider:

Task
 ↓
Required reasoning
 ↓
Context requirement
 ↓
Tool requirements
 ↓
Latency requirement
 ↓
Hardware availability
 ↓
Model health
 ↓
Privacy policy
 ↓
Cost policy
 ↓
Available model

Therefore CNS does not need a permanent rule such as:

> "Qwen is always the best."



Instead:

> Use the best available model that satisfies the task and current policy.




---

9. Regular Background Agents

Local models should be the preferred option for routine CNS work.

Examples:

Health checking
Memory maintenance
Log analysis
File organization
Agent status checking
Routine summaries
Background orchestration
Non-critical coding
Housekeeping

The general path:

Background Agent
       ↓
Local Model Bus
       ↓
Suitable local model
       ↓
Task

This keeps routine CNS activity inexpensive and reduces unnecessary remote API usage.


---

10. Research Mode

Research can use a layered strategy.

Research Task
     │
     ▼
Local Model
     │
     ├── sufficient → continue
     │
     └── insufficient
             ↓
       approved remote/free model
             ↓
       paid model if policy allows

The local model can handle reasoning, organization, summarization, preprocessing, and other appropriate work.

External search/data tools remain separate from the model itself.


---

11. Live Trading Mode

This requires a stricter architecture.

The LLM must not become the single point of failure for trading execution.

The core remains:

Market Data
    ↓
Strategy Engine
    ↓
Risk Engine
    ↓
Portfolio Engine
    ↓
Execution Engine
    ↓
Broker

LLMs can assist with:

Analysis
Research
Reasoning
Interpretation
Scenario analysis
Explanations

But:

LLM failure
     ≠
Trading system failure

And an LLM must not bypass deterministic risk controls.


---

12. Local Model Failure

If a local model crashes, CNS itself must continue running.

Possible fallback:

Local Model A
      ↓
failed
      ↓
Local Model B
      ↓
failed
      ↓
Approved remote/free model
      ↓
Approved paid model

The exact fallback order is controlled by policy.

For live trading, deterministic safety systems remain independent.


---

13. Runtime Abstraction

CNS should not permanently depend on one inference runtime.

Possible runtimes include:

llama.cpp
vLLM
SGLang
Ollama
Future compatible runtimes

The runtime sits underneath the CNS Model Bus.

Architecture:

CNS
 ↓
Model Bus
 ↓
Model Adapter
 ↓
Runtime Adapter
 ↓
llama.cpp / vLLM / SGLang / etc.
 ↓
Hardware

Therefore changing the runtime should not require changing CNS's core architecture.


---

14. Quantization Is a First-Class Capability

Quantization must be treated as part of the deployment system.

CNS should understand variants such as:

Q4
Q5
Q6
Q8
FP8
...

The registry should record the resource requirements of each variant.

For example:

Model X
 ├── Q4 → low resource
 ├── Q5 → medium resource
 ├── Q6 → higher resource
 └── Q8 → higher quality/resource requirement

CNS can select the appropriate version based on available hardware and task requirements.


